  <!-- Main Editorial Content -->
  <div class="bg-white border border-gray-200 rounded-xl p-6 sm:p-8 mb-8 prose prose-slate max-w-none">
    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900">Why Does Polymeric Haze Happen?</h2>
    <p>
      Standard sand is inert crushed quartz. Polymeric sand, by contrast, contains roughly 85% to 90% quartz sand and 10% to 15% synthetic polymers, cross-linking plasticizers, and high-early portland cement. When you sweep polymeric sand into paver joints, the fine chemical binders separate from the sand grains as an invisible airborne dust that clings to textured paver pores.
    </p>
    <p>
      The moment you spray water with a garden hose, the water activates the binders on top of the pavers instead of inside the joints. Within 20 minutes of sun exposure, those polymers solidify into a milky white resin coat that water, pressure washers, and soap cannot wash away.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8">The 5-Step Zero-Haze Jobsite Protocol</h2>
    <ol class="space-y-4 text-gray-700 not-prose my-6">
      <li class="flex gap-3 bg-gray-50 p-4 rounded-lg border border-gray-200">
        <span class="bg-blue-600 text-white font-bold rounded-full w-6 h-6 flex items-center justify-center text-xs shrink-0 mt-0.5">1</span>
        <div>
          <strong class="text-gray-900 block text-sm mb-1">Verify Paver Surface is 100% Bone Dry</strong>
          <span class="text-xs text-gray-600">Never install polymeric sand early in the morning when dew sits on the stones, or if it rained within the previous 24 hours. The slightest moisture will cause binders to stick instantly to the top surface.</span>
        </div>
      </li>
      <li class="flex gap-3 bg-gray-50 p-4 rounded-lg border border-gray-200">
        <span class="bg-blue-600 text-white font-bold rounded-full w-6 h-6 flex items-center justify-center text-xs shrink-0 mt-0.5">2</span>
        <div>
          <strong class="text-gray-900 block text-sm mb-1">Vibrate Sand Down 1/8" Below Chamfer</strong>
          <span class="text-xs text-gray-600">Run a plate compactor fitted with a neoprene mat over the dry pavers. Compaction shakes bridging sand down into joint voids. Joints must be filled to exactly 1/8 inch below the paver chamfer edge—never flush with the surface.</span>
        </div>
      </li>
      <li class="flex gap-3 bg-gray-50 p-4 rounded-lg border border-gray-200">
        <span class="bg-blue-600 text-white font-bold rounded-full w-6 h-6 flex items-center justify-center text-xs shrink-0 mt-0.5">3</span>
        <div>
          <strong class="text-gray-900 block text-sm mb-1">Low-Throttle Leaf Blower Sweep (The Secret Weapon)</strong>
          <span class="text-xs text-gray-600">After sweeping excess sand away with a soft bristle broom, take a cordless leaf blower set to low speed. Hold the nozzle at waist height, angled slightly upward. Blow all airborne dust particles off the paver surface without dislodging sand inside the joints.</span>
        </div>
      </li>
      <li class="flex gap-3 bg-gray-50 p-4 rounded-lg border border-gray-200">
        <span class="bg-blue-600 text-white font-bold rounded-full w-6 h-6 flex items-center justify-center text-xs shrink-0 mt-0.5">4</span>
        <div>
          <strong class="text-gray-900 block text-sm mb-1">3-Stage Gentle Water Misting</strong>
          <span class="text-xs text-gray-600">Set your hose nozzle to 'MIST' or 'SHOWER' (never JET). Spray a 200 sq ft section for 15 seconds until water glistens. Wait 5 minutes for water to soak 1 inch down. Repeat a second and third time until sand is saturated 2 to 3 inches deep.</span>
        </div>
      </li>
      <li class="flex gap-3 bg-gray-50 p-4 rounded-lg border border-gray-200">
        <span class="bg-blue-600 text-white font-bold rounded-full w-6 h-6 flex items-center justify-center text-xs shrink-0 mt-0.5">5</span>
        <div>
          <strong class="text-gray-900 block text-sm mb-1">Blow Off Water Puddles Immediately</strong>
          <span class="text-xs text-gray-600">Once saturated, use the leaf blower again to blow standing water puddles off the pavers into surrounding grass or gravel. Puddled water contains dissolved polymers that dry into white rings if left in the sun.</span>
        </div>
      </li>
    </ol>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8">How to Remove Polymeric Haze if it Already Happened</h2>
    <p>
      If your pavers already dried with a cloudy white haze, hot water and dawn dish soap will not dissolve cured polyurethane resins. You need a dedicated <strong>polymeric haze remover</strong> containing buffered sulfamic acid or glycolic acid (such as Alliance Gator Clean or SRW Products Haze Remover).
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Avoid Raw Muriatic Acid:</strong> Unbuffered muriatic acid eats the cement cream layer on concrete pavers, permanently etching colors and exposing coarse aggregate beneath.</li>
      <li><strong>Test on an Inconspicuous Corner:</strong> Apply specialized haze remover to a 2x2 test patch with a stiff-bristle nylon deck brush, allow to dwell for 5 to 7 minutes, scrub vigorously, and rinse thoroughly with a 2,500 PSI pressure washer.</li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  <!-- Jobsite Field Note -->
  <FieldNote
    author="David Miller, PE"
    role="Structural & Civil Engineering Consultant"
    experience="20+ Years Field Experience"
    avatarBg="bg-blue-100 text-blue-800"
    initials="DM"
    title="The 1/8-Inch Joint Chamfer Rule"
    note="The single biggest mistake homeowners make is filling paver joints flush with the top surface. High-end architectural pavers have a beveled chamfer edge. When you fill sand flush with the bevel, shoe soles and patio chair legs grind against the top 1/8 inch of sand every day. This grinds the hardened polymer back into fine dust that tracks across the stones and gets washed across the surface by summer rains. Always keep the sand 1/8 inch below the bottom of the chamfer."
  />

